{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And perform the levirate marriage with her, and raise up seed for your brother.</b> The son is called by the name of the deceased — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 38:8). And this is not true, for in the commandment of the Torah it is likewise stated (Deuteronomy 25:6): \"He shall rise up on the name of his deceased brother, and his name shall not be blotted out from Israel,\" yet the levir is not commanded to name his son with the name of his deceased brother. And concerning Boaz it says: \"and also Ruth the Moabitess, the wife of Mahlon, I have acquired to me as a wife, to raise up the name of the deceased upon his inheritance, and that the name of the deceased shall not be cut off from among his brethren and from the gate of his place\" — and they called him Obed, not Mahlon (Ruth 4:10). Furthermore, it says: \"And Onan knew that the seed would not be his,\" and what harm would come upon him, that he destroyed his seed on her account, if his son were to be called by the name of his deceased brother, when most people desire to do so? And Scripture does not say \"And Onan said,\" but rather \"And Onan knew that the seed would not be his,\" for he had clear knowledge of this, that the seed would not be his.\n\nBut the matter is a great secret among the secrets of the Torah concerning the generations of man, and it is visible to the eyes of seers to whom HaShem has given eyes to see and ears to hear. And the early sages, prior to the giving of the Torah, knew that there is great benefit in the levirate marriage of the brother, and that he is the most fitting to take precedence in it, and after him the relative in the family, for any close relation of his from his family who inherits the inheritance will produce benefit from him. And they would practice marrying the wife of the deceased — the brother, or the father, or the relative from the family — and we do not know whether this practice predated Judah. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 85:5) they said that Judah was the first to begin the mitzvah of levirate marriage, for when he received the secret from his ancestors he hastened to establish it, and when the Torah came and forbade the wives of certain relatives, HaShem wished to permit the prohibition of a brother's wife for the sake of levirate marriage, but did not wish to set aside on its account the prohibition of a paternal uncle's wife and a son's wife and others, for with a brother the matter was customary and the benefit is close at hand, but not with them, as I have mentioned. And behold, it is reckoned as great cruelty in a brother when he does not wish to perform levirate marriage, and they call him \"the house of the unsandaled one\" (Deuteronomy 25:10), for now he has drawn away from them, and it is fitting that this mitzvah be performed through the drawing off of the shoe. And the early sages of Israel, from their own knowledge of this weighty matter, established in former times in Israel the practice of performing this act with all heirs to the inheritance, among those in whom there is no prohibition of close kinship, and they called it redemption (גאולה), and this is the matter of Boaz and the reasoning of Naomi and the neighbors. And the enlightened will understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:46.302299569Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}